## Core idea

A binding owns a value; moving that value transfers responsibility for its eventual destruction. Moving a String normally transfers its descriptor, not a copy of its heap bytes. Copy types instead permit implicit duplication. Scope determines when owned values are dropped, while borrow checking prevents references from being used after their owners become unavailable. Stack versus heap placement alone does not determine ownership.

## Worked example

After `let a = String::from("tick"); let b = a;`, b owns the allocation and a cannot be used. Calling `inspect(&b)` lends access; calling `consume(b)` transfers ownership. Cloning b explicitly creates another owned string.

### Follow the steps

Trace this sequence before compiling it.

~~~rust
fn inspect(s: &str) { println!("{}", s.len()); }
fn normalize(mut s: String) -> String {
    s.make_ascii_lowercase();
    s
}
fn main() {
    let a = String::from("TICK");
    let b = a;
    inspect(&b);
    let c = normalize(b);
    assert_eq!(c, "tick");
}
~~~

The allocation moves from a to b, then through normalize to c. inspect borrows it and leaves b available. Uncomment a use of a after the assignment or a use of b after normalize, one at a time, to see the compiler reject each moved-from binding.

## Try it

Sketch `inspect`, `consume`, and `normalize` signatures. Make normalize return ownership when it creates new bytes. Trace the owner before and after each call.

## Check your work

A borrowed inspector accepts `&str`; a consumer may accept String. Explain where each allocation is destroyed and why a reference cannot keep a moved or destroyed value alive.

## Primary reading

Optional reading after the example and exercise.

- [The Rust Book, What Is Ownership?](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-01-what-is-ownership.html) · Focus on moves and the return of ownership from functions.
